Confirmed: the template filename never carries the design name. For GMR⁴ with 6 designs that's a real problem (six files named "GMR4 数据模板_时间戳.xlsx"). 

Fix: pass a design tag. `templateFilename(slug, stamp)` — I don't want to change sheetIo's signature for all modules (risk). Better: in GMR4's `templateFileName()`, append the design id/short label:

```js
    templateFileName() {
      // 文件名要能认出是哪份设计：六个模板都叫"GMR4 数据模板_时间戳.xlsx"的话，
      // 用户下载三份就分不清谁是谁（实测就是这个状态）。
      const base = templateFilename('GMR4', this.stamp());
      const id = this.selectedTplId;
      if (!id) return base;
      return base.replace(/\.xlsx$/, `_设计${id}.xlsx`);
    }
```
Hmm, better human-readable: use the design's short label? Filenames with Chinese + × characters might be awkward but the project already uses Chinese filenames (GMR4 数据模板_...). Let me check existing fileStamp/naming conventions and whether other export filenames embed Chinese — yes: `GMR4 输入_...json`. So Chinese is fine.

Use a compact design tag: `${tpl.spec.S}秩${tpl.spec.n}样本`? Simpler and unambiguous: the id itself is descriptive (lowrank3/rank1/fullrank/confoundedZ/smallN/manyResp). I'll do `GMR4 数据模板_时间戳_rank1.xlsx`, plus note in the toast. Hmm, users may prefer Chinese: 'GMR4 数据模板_20261120_rank1（真实秩 1）.xlsx' — parentheses/spaces in filenames are OK but risky for downstream code. Keep it clean: append the id only.

Wait — careful about assertions elsewhere: test may check `/^GMR4 .*\.xlsx$/` for export jobs (that's planExport, different path) and section 7 checks `/templateFilename\('GMR4'/` in view — my change keeps that call ✓.

